<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Svatopluk I of Moravia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 840–894</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Svatopluk the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Svatopluk I was ruler of Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> from 870 to 894, under whom the state reached its greatest extent, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, parts of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>. He came to power by betraying his uncle Rastislav to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and later favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> clergy, expelling the disciples of Methodius after 885. After his death his sons quarrelled and Hungarian attacks destroyed the state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/svatopluk-i-of-moravia.jpg" alt="Svatopluk I of Moravia" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ján Kulich (sculptor) - cc-by-3.0 Peter Zelizňák(photo) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bratislava_Profesionalita_maestra_Kulicha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
